  ],
  "text": "An ordinary fluffy cloud weighs about five hundred tonnes. Anything that heavy should already be on the ground. But a cloud is not one heavy thing. It is trillions of tiny ones. Each droplet is a millionth of a metre across. To something that small, air is thick. So they are falling. Just at about one centimetre per second. And underneath them, warm air is rising faster than they can drop. Rain only happens when droplets crash together and finally get too big to hold up. So the cloud never stopped falling. It has just been losing the whole time. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
